# wy/wyrm/compiler/expressions.wy - epic 10 M2
#
# Wyrm port of pypoc/wypoc/compiler_bc/expressions.py: expression lowering
# (spec 7.1).
#
# Every handler has the same contract:
#
#     compile_expr(node, frame, dst = nil) -> register reference | error
#
# It returns the register the value ends up in, or an `error` (this codebase
# has no exceptions - every fallible step is checked with `is error` and
# propagated, the same convention context.wy/analysis.wy already use). With
# `dst` given the value *must* land there; with `dst` nil the handler is free
# to return a register that already holds the value - a local or a parameter
# needs no code at all - or to push a temp and evaluate into it.
#
# Temp discipline follows Appendix A (pypoc/doc/llm-bytecode.md, "Lowering
# recipes"): operands are pushed, and a result is pushed *above* them rather
# than reusing their slots, so `"Hello " + name` lands its operand in L0 and
# its sum in L1. The whole run of temps is released at the end of the
# enclosing statement or call window.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deviations from expressions.py, each forced by the port
#
# 1. Literals arrive pre-decoded. wy/wyrm/parser.wy already calls
#    decode_str/decode_int/decode_float/decode_sym at parse time (see
#    parser.wy:110-125), so a `str`/`int`/`float`/`sym` node's field 1 is
#    already the decoded native value - not raw token text the way
#    expressions.py's ast.Str/ast.Num/ast.Symbol carry it. So this port
#    never calls eval_string_literal/eval_number_literal/eval_char_literal:
#    there is nothing left to decode, and decode.wy's own decode_str has a
#    different (richer) escape table than the interpreter's anyway - using
#    it here would be exactly the second-copy-of-the-escape-table problem
#    expressions.py's docstring warns against, not a fix for it.
#
# 2. Char literals are unreachable. wy/wyrm/parser.wy's `char_literal()`
#    (parser.wy:136-137) is `@accept 'TOK_LITERAL_CHAR` with no value
#    transform - no `_mk_char` exists, so a char literal never becomes a
#    `$['char, ...]` node (or any node at all) in the tree wy/wyrm/ast.wy
#    documents. A `'char` handler is still provided defensively (assuming a
#    future fix decodes into field 1 the same pre-decoded way str/int/sym
#    do), but nothing can reach it today. Real parser gap, not this port's
#    to fix (M2 touches expressions.wy only).
#
# 3. Unary `pos` (`+x`) has no VM opcode. pypoc's own UNARYOPS table has no
#    "pos" entry either (its parser folds unary `+` away entirely); wy/wyrm's
#    parser keeps a `'pos` node, and the interpreter's `_expr_unaryop`
#    answers `+val` for it - which is `val` unchanged for every primitive
#    type the language has. So `'pos` lowers as an identity passthrough
#    (compile the operand, land it in dst): correct per the interpreter,
#    with no opcode to spend.
#
# 4. `and`/`or` are flat n-ary chains in wy/wyrm (ast.wy: DIVERGES, pypoc's
#    BinOp is strictly binary and a chain nests). `_short_circuit` here folds
#    left to right across every operand, emitting one conditional jump to a
#    shared `done` label before each operand after the first - the direct
#    generalization of pypoc's binary version, and what a left-associative
#    reading of the chain means operationally either way.
#
# 5. `(a, b) ! name(args)` (pypoc's ast.MessageTupleExpr, a dedicated node)
#    has no dedicated wy/wyrm node kind: `(expr_list)` parses as an ordinary
#    `'tuple` node (parser.wy:1202), and message_op's target is just
#    whatever primary preceded it. So `_message` below detects a `'tuple`
#    target itself and takes the receivers-tuple path, subsuming
#    `_message_tuple` - matching pypoc's *tree-walking interpreter*
#    (wyrm_eval_parse_tree.py), which the epic's approved deviation prefers
#    over the retired bytecode VM's node-type distinction.
#
# 6. `super` and a qualified message name (`recv ! mod::name(...)`) are not
#    reachable: wy/wyrm's grammar has no `super` construct at all (no
#    `TOK_SUPER`, no rule), and `message_op`'s name is a single `xid`, never
#    a `(:: ...)` path. Neither `_super` nor a
#    module-qualified `message_index` is implemented; both are real parser
#    gaps, not lowering gaps, and out of scope for expressions.wy.
#
# 7. `do:` blocks and lambdas break a real dependency cycle, closed with a
#    hook rather than an import. `_do` needs statements.wy's `compile_body`
#    (M3) and `_lambda` needs functions.wy's `compile_closure_expr` (M4) -
#    pypoc breaks this same cycle with a function-local `import` at call
#    time (its `_lambda`/`_do` docstrings say so explicitly); wyrm has no
#    equivalent of a deferred/lazy import, and wy/wyrm's import graph must
#    stay acyclic besides (pypoc/doc/addendum.md), so this file cannot
#    import statements.wy the way statements.wy imports it. As of M3, `_do`
#    calls through `set_body_compiler`'s one-shot hook instead (see below);
#    `_lambda` is still stubbed, for M4 to close the same way.
#
# 8. `foo::$ast` (`_ast_ref`) has no grammar of its own: `$` is a legal
#    identifier character, so `foo::$ast` parses as an ordinary
#    `(:: foo $ast)` path whose leaf segment spells `$ast`, and it is the
#    lowering (`_scope`) that decides - by that leaf spelling - that this
#    names a definition's tree rather than a scope hop. The tree comes from
#    `frame.module.definitions`, populated by module.wy's declare pass
#    (10a M0; M3 will record post-expansion trees there instead), and is
#    materialized only at a reference site, via `emit_value` - the same
#    tree-constant path `$[...]` literals use.
#
# 9. `_scope`'s free-global/class-static shortcut is checked once, against
#    the whole flattened `(:: ...)` path, then folds the remaining
#    segments with `getscope`. pypoc's `_scope` re-derives this check at
#    *each* nesting level (`ast.Scope` is binary and `compile_expr` recurses
#    into `node.obj`), which only matters when an inner prefix of a 3+
#    segment path is itself eligible for the class-static/free-global
#    shortcut (a local class reached partway through a longer path) - not
#    exercised by any progression fixture. Documented approximation, not
#    silently different behavior for the 1- and 2-segment cases that matter.

import std::pairs::*
import wyrm::opcodes::*
import wyrm::compiler::context::*
import wyrm::compiler::analysis::*
import wyrm::compiler::handlers::*

INT32_MIN := -(2 ** 31)
INT32_MAX := (2 ** 31) - 1

# spec 3.2/3.3: the three-address op each binary operator lowers to. Keyed
# by the operator's spelling (str), valued by the opcode mnemonic (sym) -
# `'exp`/`'in` are their own wy/wyrm node kinds, not routed through here,
# but "**"/"in" stay in the table since nothing else needs to change if
# that ever stops being true.
BINOPS := {"+": 'add, "-": 'sub, "*": 'mul, "/": 'div, "%": 'mod, "**": 'pow, "&": 'band, "|": 'bor, "^": 'bxor, "<<": 'shl, ">>": 'shr, "==": 'eq, "!=": 'ne, "<": 'lt, "<=": 'le, ">": 'gt, ">=": 'ge, "<=>": 'cmp3, "in": 'in}

# `x is T`: the names the interpreter answers from its own primitive-type
# table rather than by looking up a class (_matches_type,
# wyrm_eval_parse_tree.py's _PRIMITIVE_TYPE_CHECKS). Frozen here for the
# same reason context.wy's BUILTIN_NAMES is: a wyrm program cannot query a
# Python dict at compile time.
PRIMITIVE_TYPE_NAMES := ["nil", "bool", "int", "uint", "float", "str", "sym", "list", "tuple", "dict", "bytes", "pair", "error"]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# small local helpers
#
# `_field`/`_tail` are analysis.wy's private helpers, copied rather than
# imported (wy has no cross-module access to a leading-underscore name, and
# even if it did: std::pairs' `list_tail`/`field` are not used here for the
# same reason analysis.wy avoids them - see its header comment). Plain
# `for x in <pair>` IS safe on the C VM (src/iter.c handles WY_TYPE_TAG_PAIR
# natively; the broken case is specifically `for i in range(...)`, a
# coroutine), so this port uses it freely for iterating argument/item lists.

fn _tail(node, count: int):
    cur := node
    i := 0
    while i < count:
        if not (cur is pair):
            return error("index out of range")
        cur = cdr(cur)
        i = i + 1
    return cur

fn _field(node, index: int):
    rest := _tail(node, index)
    if rest is error:
        return rest
    return car(rest)

fn _max2(a: int, b: int) -> int:
    if a > b:
        return a
    return b

fn _join_parts(parts: list) -> str:
    out := ""
    first := true
    for part in parts:
        if not first:
            out = out + "::"
        out = out + part
        first = false
    return out

# `bytes!pack_f32` truncates to the machine's native (single-precision)
# float and `unpack_u32` reads the same bits back - the wyrm-side
# equivalent of expressions.py's `struct.unpack("<I", struct.pack("<f",
# value))`.
fn _f32_bits(value: float) -> int:
    b := bytes(4)
    b ! pack_f32(0, value)
    return b ! unpack_u32(0)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# emit helpers: pack (or pack_pairable) THEN emit, checking the error each
# can return in one place instead of at every call site.

fn _emit_pack(frame: FnContext, op, f: int = 0, a0: int = 0, a1: int = 0, a2: int = 0, w1: int | nil = nil) -> nil | error:
    words := pack(op, f=f, a0=a0, a1=a1, a2=a2, w1=w1)
    if words is error:
        return words
    frame ! emit_words(words)
    return nil

fn _emit_pairable(frame: FnContext, op, primary: int = 0, secondary: int = 0) -> nil | error:
    words := pack_pairable(op, primary, secondary)
    if words is error:
        return words
    frame ! emit_words(words)
    return nil

# Put a value already in `reg` into `dst`, if the caller demanded one.
fn _land(frame: FnContext, dst, reg: int) -> int | error:
    if (dst is nil) or (dst == reg):
        return reg
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'move, dst, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return dst

fn _target(frame: FnContext, dst) -> int | error:
    if dst is nil:
        return frame ! push()
    return dst

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# literals

fn _string(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    index := (frame.module.image) ! add_static(value)
    if index is error:
        return index
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'lconst, index, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _number(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    if value is float:
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        bits := _f32_bits(value)
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'f32, a0=reg, w1=bits)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if (value < INT32_MIN) or (value > INT32_MAX):
        return error("integer literal " + str(value) + " does not fit in an i32")
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'i8, reg, value)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# Defensive only - see deviation 2: no `'char` node reaches this today.
fn _char_lit(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'i8, reg, value)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _symbol_lit(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    index := (frame.module.image) ! add_symbol(str(value))
    if index is error:
        return index
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'lsym, index, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _bool_lit(flag: bool, frame: FnContext, dst):
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    f := 0
    if flag:
        f = 1
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lbool, a0=reg, f=f)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# names (the resolution ladder, spec 6.3)

fn reg_of(where: list) -> int:
    return where[1]

fn _land_free(frame: FnContext, dst, slotreg: int):
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'gget, slotreg, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# Lower a bare identifier: local, then parameter/capture, then module
# global, then a free global slotreg (spec 6.3). Nothing is ever resolved
# statically across a module boundary.
fn resolve_name(name, frame: FnContext, dst):
    where := frame ! storage(name)
    if not (where is nil):
        kind := where[0]
        slotreg := where[1]
        if kind == "reg":
            if name in frame.cells:
                # The register holds the box, not the value (spec 8.3).
                out := _target(frame, dst)
                if out is error:
                    return out
                z := frame ! zero()
                if z is error:
                    return z
                e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getidx, a0=out, a1=reg_of(where), a2=z)
                if e is error:
                    return e
                return out
            return _land(frame, dst, slotreg)
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        if kind == "slot":
            e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getattr, a0=reg, a1=frame.this_reg, a2=slotreg)
            if e is error:
                return e
        else:
            e2 := _emit_pairable(frame, 'gget, slotreg, reg)
            if e2 is error:
                return e2
        return reg
    module := frame.module
    if not (module.session_declared is nil):
        # REPL session: an unknown name may be defined by a later input, so it
        # is a forward reference (a fillable slot), never "undefined".
        if not (module ! is_builtin(name)):
            fwd := module ! declare_forward_global(name)
            if fwd is error:
                return fwd
            return _land_free(frame, dst, fwd)
    elif (not (module ! is_builtin(name))) and (not module.has_wildcard_import):
        return error("undefined name '" + str(name) + "'")
    free := module ! declare_free_global(str(name))
    if free is error:
        return free
    return _land_free(frame, dst, free)

# `(nil)`, the literal nil (ast.md R3).
fn _nil_lit(frame: FnContext, dst):
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _this(frame: FnContext, dst):
    if frame.this_reg is nil:
        return error("`this` is only meaningful inside a method")
    return _land(frame, dst, frame.this_reg)

# `node` here is a bare symbol, which is always a name (ast.md R3): `this`,
# which the enclosing method binds (syntax.md G4), or a name to resolve.
fn _bare_symbol(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    if node == 'this:
        return _this(frame, dst)
    return resolve_name(node, frame, dst)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# operators

fn _unary(node, frame: FnContext, dst, op):
    operand := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    src := compile_expr(operand, frame, nil)
    if src is error:
        return src
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, op, reg, src)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# See deviation 3: `+x` is an identity passthrough, no opcode to spend.
fn _pos(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    operand := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    src := compile_expr(operand, frame, nil)
    if src is error:
        return src
    return _land(frame, dst, src)

# `(op lhs rhs)` (ast.md R5).
fn _binop(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    op_sym := car(node)
    lhs_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    rhs_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    op := (BINOPS[str(op_sym)] catch nil)
    if op is nil:
        return error("the bytecode compiler does not support the '" + str(op_sym) + "' operator yet")
    lhs := compile_expr(lhs_node, frame, nil)
    if lhs is error:
        return lhs
    rhs := compile_expr(rhs_node, frame, nil)
    if rhs is error:
        return rhs
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, op, a0=reg, a1=lhs, a2=rhs)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _exp(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    base_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    exp_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    lhs := compile_expr(base_node, frame, nil)
    if lhs is error:
        return lhs
    rhs := compile_expr(exp_node, frame, nil)
    if rhs is error:
        return rhs
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'pow, a0=reg, a1=lhs, a2=rhs)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _in_op(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    target_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    lhs := compile_expr(value_node, frame, nil)
    if lhs is error:
        return lhs
    rhs := compile_expr(target_node, frame, nil)
    if rhs is error:
        return rhs
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'in, a0=reg, a1=lhs, a2=rhs)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# `a and b and c ...`/`a or b or c ...`: the result register holds the
# first operand, and each later one only overwrites it when the chain has
# not already decided the answer (spec 7.1). See deviation 4.
fn _short_circuit(node, frame: FnContext, dst, is_and: bool):
    operands := (_tail(node, 1) catch nil)
    if not (operands is pair):
        return error("'and'/'or' needs at least one operand")
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    mark := frame ! mark()
    r := compile_expr(car(operands), frame, reg)
    if r is error:
        return r
    fe := frame ! free_to(mark)
    if fe is error:
        return fe
    done := frame ! new_label()
    op := 'jf
    if not is_and:
        op = 'jt
    rest := cdr(operands)
    while rest is pair:
        e := frame ! emit_jump(op, done, reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        r2 := compile_expr(car(rest), frame, reg)
        if r2 is error:
            return r2
        fe2 := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fe2 is error:
            return fe2
        rest = cdr(rest)
    ml := frame ! mark_label(done)
    if ml is error:
        return ml
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `is` (type checks, spec 7.1)

# The atoms of a type node: itself for a plain type `(type a)`, and for a
# union `(type a b ...)` (ast.md) each member as its own `(type a)`.
fn _type_atoms(type_node) -> list | error:
    if (not (type_node is pair)) or (car(type_node) != 'type):
        return error("not a type expression")
    atoms := (_tail(type_node, 1) catch nil)
    if (atoms is pair) and (cdr(atoms) is pair):
        out2 := []
        while atoms is pair:
            out2 ! append($['type, car(atoms)])
            atoms = cdr(atoms)
        return out2
    return [type_node]

fn _type_parts(atom_node) -> list | error:
    if (not (atom_node is pair)) or (car(atom_node) != 'type):
        return error("not a type atom")
    x := (_field(atom_node, 1) catch nil)
    if x is sym:
        return [str(x)]
    if is_path_node(x):
        out := []
        rest := (_tail(x, 1) catch nil)
        while rest is pair:
            out ! append(str(car(rest)))
            rest = cdr(rest)
        return out
    return error("unsupported type atom shape")

# Load the class a type annotation names - a primitive type as its *name*
# (a string constant, spec 6.3), anything else resolved as an ordinary name
# or, past one segment, a free global slotreg (never resolved across a module
# boundary at compile time).
fn _type_value(atom_node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    parts := _type_parts(atom_node)
    if parts is error:
        return parts
    if len(parts) == 0:
        return error("empty type expression")
    last := parts[len(parts) - 1]
    if last in PRIMITIVE_TYPE_NAMES:
        index := (frame.module.image) ! add_static(last)
        if index is error:
            return index
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'lconst, index, reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if len(parts) == 1:
        return resolve_name(sym(parts[0]), frame, dst)
    free := (frame.module) ! declare_free_global(_join_parts(parts))
    if free is error:
        return free
    return _land_free(frame, dst, free)

fn _type_check(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    type_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    atoms := _type_atoms(type_node)
    if atoms is error:
        return atoms
    value := compile_expr(value_node, frame, nil)
    if value is error:
        return value
    var types_reg: int
    if len(atoms) == 1:
        r := _type_value(atoms[0], frame, nil)
        if r is error:
            return r
        types_reg = r
    else:
        base := frame ! mark()
        for atom in atoms:
            slotreg := frame ! push()
            if slotreg is error:
                return slotreg
            r2 := _type_value(atom, frame, slotreg)
            if r2 is error:
                return r2
        top := frame ! push()
        if top is error:
            return top
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'tuple, a0=top, a1=L(base), f=len(atoms))
        if e is error:
            return e
        types_reg = top
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, 'is, a0=reg, a1=value, a2=types_reg)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# calls

# Lower `node` so its value lands in the next free temp slotreg - what a call
# window's argument slots need. A value already sitting at the top of the
# temp stack needs no copy; anything else (a local, a parameter, a lower
# temp) is copied up.
fn compile_into_window(node, frame: FnContext) -> int | error:
    slotreg := frame ! mark()
    reg := compile_expr(node, frame, nil)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    if (reg == slotreg) and ((frame ! mark()) > slotreg):
        return reg
    fe := frame ! free_to(slotreg)
    if fe is error:
        return fe
    target := frame ! push()
    if target is error:
        return target
    e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'move, target, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return target

fn has_spread_or_keyword(args) -> bool:
    for arg in args:
        kind := node_kind(arg)
        if (kind == 'kwarg) or (kind == 'spread) or (kind == 'spread_kw):
            return true
    return false

fn _compile_call(node, frame: FnContext, nres: int) -> int | error:
    func_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    args := (_tail(node, 2) catch nil)
    argc := len(args)
    if argc > 128:
        return error(str(argc) + " arguments, over the 128-parameter call limit")
    if nres > 128:
        return error(str(nres) + " results wanted, over the 128-result call limit")
    base := frame ! mark()
    window := frame ! push()
    if window is error:
        return window
    if func_node is sym:
        r := resolve_name(func_node, frame, window)
        if r is error:
            return r
    else:
        r2 := compile_expr(func_node, frame, window)
        if r2 is error:
            return r2
    for arg in args:
        w := compile_into_window(arg, frame)
        if w is error:
            return w
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'call, a0=window, f=argc, a1=nres)
    if e is error:
        return e
    held := base + _max2(nres, 1)
    while (frame ! mark()) < held:
        p := frame ! push()
        if p is error:
            return p
    fr := frame ! free_to(held)
    if fr is error:
        return fr
    return window

# `trap([code])`: an intrinsic, not a function. Lowers to the `trap` opcode,
# whose `code` is an 8-bit immediate - hence an integer *literal* 0-255
# (default 1, "debugger break"; 0 is what the compiler itself emits for a
# body it could not lower; 2-255 are the program's own). The fault ends the
# call, so the instructions after it are dead: `nres` registers are still
# reserved (and one is nil-filled) only so callers that expect a window keep
# their register discipline. It is recognised only as a call whose name is
# not bound (a local, parameter, capture or module global named `trap`
# shadows it), and never as a value.
fn _compile_trap(node, frame: FnContext, nres: int) -> int | error:
    args := (_tail(node, 2) catch nil)
    code := 1
    if len(args) > 1:
        return error("trap takes at most one argument, an integer literal 0-255")
    if len(args) == 1:
        arg := args[0]
        if (arg is sym) or (node_kind(arg) != 'int):
            return error("trap's argument must be an integer literal 0-255")
        code = (_field(arg, 1) catch -1)
        if (code < 0) or (code > 255):
            return error("trap's argument must be an integer literal 0-255")
    base := frame ! mark()
    window := frame ! push()
    if window is error:
        return window
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'trap, f=code)
    if e is error:
        return e
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=window)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    held := base + _max2(nres, 1)
    while (frame ! mark()) < held:
        p := frame ! push()
        if p is error:
            return p
    fr := frame ! free_to(held)
    if fr is error:
        return fr
    return window

fn compile_call(node, frame: FnContext, nres: int) -> int | error:
    func_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    if (func_node is sym) and (func_node == 'trap) and ((frame ! storage(func_node)) is nil):
        return _compile_trap(node, frame, nres)
    args := (_tail(node, 2) catch nil)
    if has_spread_or_keyword(args):
        func_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
        return compile_va(frame, nres, 'call_va, func_node, args, nil, nil)
    return _compile_call(node, frame, nres)

# An application is `(apply callee arg...)` (ast.md R5): a direct send when
# the callee is `(bind_msg recv sel)` (_message), otherwise a call. The
# arguments are the apply's tail.
fn _call(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    r := compile_call(node, frame, 1)
    if r is error:
        return r
    return _land(frame, dst, r)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# collections (spec 7.1)

fn _window_of(items, frame: FnContext) -> list | error:
    base := frame ! mark()
    for item in items:
        w := compile_into_window(item, frame)
        if w is error:
            return w
    return [L(base), len(items)]

fn _sequence(frame: FnContext, dst, op, items, limit: int | nil):
    n := len(items)
    if (not (limit is nil)) and (n > limit):
        return error(str(n) + " elements, over the " + str(limit) + "-element " + str(op) + " limit")
    win := _window_of(items, frame)
    if win is error:
        return win
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, op, a0=reg, a1=win[0], f=win[1])
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

fn _array(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    items := (_tail(node, 1) catch nil)
    return _sequence(frame, dst, 'list, items, nil)

fn _tuple(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    items := (_tail(node, 1) catch nil)
    return _sequence(frame, dst, 'tuple, items, 128)

fn _plist(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    items := (_tail(node, 1) catch nil)
    return _sequence(frame, dst, 'plist, items, nil)

# `dict` reads a window of k, v, k, v..., so the entries are flattened into
# one run of temps (spec 3.3). Each entry is a bare 2-element list
# `$[key, value]` (ast.wy: no head symbol).
fn _dict(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    entries := (_tail(node, 1) catch nil)
    flattened := []
    count := 0
    for entry in entries:
        key := car(entry)
        value := car(cdr(entry))
        flattened ! append(key)
        flattened ! append(value)
        count = count + 1
    win := _window_of(flattened, frame)
    if win is error:
        return win
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'dict, a0=reg, a1=win[0], f=count)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# object access

fn own_slot_index(node, frame: FnContext):
    obj_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    if (obj_node != 'this) or (frame.this_class is nil):
        return nil
    if not frame.module.slot_optimization:
        return nil
    name := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    return (frame.module) ! slot_index(frame.this_class, name)

fn _attr(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    slotreg := own_slot_index(node, frame)
    obj_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    obj := compile_expr(obj_node, frame, nil)
    if obj is error:
        return obj
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    if not (slotreg is nil):
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getslot, a0=reg, a1=obj, a2=slotreg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    name := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    symbol := (frame.module.image) ! add_symbol(str(name))
    if symbol is error:
        return symbol
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, 'getattr, a0=reg, a1=obj, a2=symbol)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    return reg

fn _index(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    obj_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    idx_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    obj := compile_expr(obj_node, frame, nil)
    if obj is error:
        return obj
    index := compile_expr(idx_node, frame, nil)
    if index is error:
        return index
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getidx, a0=reg, a1=obj, a2=index)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# closures and inlined blocks - see deviation 7.
#
# `_do` is wired up as of M3, but not by an `import`: wy/wyrm's import graph
# must stay acyclic (pypoc/doc/addendum.md, "Import cycles are illegal"), so
# this file cannot `import wyrm::compiler::statements::*` - statements.wy
# already imports this one. Instead this is a one-shot hook:
# `statements.wy` calls `set_body_compiler(compile_body)` once it loads, at
# its own module's bottom. Anything that wants `do:` blocks to lower has to
# import `wyrm::compiler::statements` (directly or transitively) before
# compiling, which installs the hook as a side effect of that import - the
# same shape pypoc's function-local `import` gives `_do`/`_lambda` there,
# adapted to wy having no lazy/deferred import of its own.
#
# `_lambda` is wired the same way as of M4: `functions.wy` calls
# `set_closure_compiler(compile_closure_expr)` at its own bottom, once
# `compile_closure_expr` exists. Anything that wants a lambda/`co_lambda`
# to lower has to import `wyrm::compiler::functions` (directly or
# transitively) before compiling, the same requirement `_do` already has on
# `wyrm::compiler::statements`.
_body_compiler := nil
_closure_compiler := nil

fn set_body_compiler(compiler) -> nil:
    _body_compiler = compiler
    return nil

fn set_closure_compiler(compiler) -> nil:
    _closure_compiler = compiler
    return nil

fn _lambda(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    if _closure_compiler is nil:
        return error("closure lowering needs functions.wy's compile_closure_expr (M4); import wyrm::compiler::functions before compiling a module with lambdas")
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    return _closure_compiler(node, frame, reg, "<lambda>")

fn _do(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    if _body_compiler is nil:
        return error("'do' block lowering needs statements.wy's compile_body; import wyrm::compiler::statements before compiling a module with 'do' blocks")
    # The `do` node is its own body and scope owner (analysis.wy's
    # body_statements/block_bodies), in either shape.
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _body_compiler(node, frame, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# error flow (spec 7.1)

# `try e`: hand an error straight back to the caller, keeping the value
# otherwise. `return` is what runs the frame's defers, so this is a real
# return rather than a jump to the epilogue.
fn _try(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    mark := frame ! mark()
    r := compile_expr(value_node, frame, reg)
    if r is error:
        return r
    fe := frame ! free_to(mark)
    if fe is error:
        return fe
    ok := frame ! new_label()
    e := frame ! emit_jump('jnerr, ok, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    e2 := frame ! emit_return(reg, 1)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    e3 := frame ! mark_label(ok)
    if e3 is error:
        return e3
    return reg

# `e catch h` replaces an error with the handler's value; `e catch return v`
# returns `v` from the enclosing function instead.
fn _catch(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    value_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    handler_node := (_field(node, 2) catch nil)
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    mark := frame ! mark()
    r := compile_expr(value_node, frame, reg)
    if r is error:
        return r
    fe := frame ! free_to(mark)
    if fe is error:
        return fe
    done := frame ! new_label()
    e := frame ! emit_jump('jnerr, done, reg)
    if e is error:
        return e
    if node_kind(handler_node) == 'return:
        rv_node := (_field(handler_node, 1) catch nil)
        var out: int
        if rv_node is nil:
            p := frame ! push()
            if p is error:
                return p
            pe := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=p)
            if pe is error:
                return pe
            out = p
        else:
            o := compile_expr(rv_node, frame, nil)
            if o is error:
                return o
            out = o
        er := frame ! emit_return(out, 1)
        if er is error:
            return er
        fe2 := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fe2 is error:
            return fe2
    else:
        r2 := compile_expr(handler_node, frame, reg)
        if r2 is error:
            return r2
        fe3 := frame ! free_to(mark)
        if fe3 is error:
            return fe3
    ml := frame ! mark_label(done)
    if ml is error:
        return ml
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# messages and namespaces

# The `messages` entry a message name binds through - spec 4.8: a name
# with no prior identity creates one. See deviation 6: a qualified message
# name has no grammar support, so `path` is always the single name.
fn message_index(node, frame: FnContext) -> int | error:
    name := (_field(_msg_binding(node), 2) catch nil)
    return frame ! reference([str(name)])

# Lay out a message window and emit `msg`; returns the window base. The
# receiver sits at the base and the arguments follow it, exactly as a
# call's callee does.
# The `(bind_msg recv sel)` of a send `(apply (bind_msg recv sel) arg...)`,
# or the node itself when it is the bound message.
fn _msg_binding(node):
    if node_kind(node) == 'apply:
        return (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    return node

fn compile_message(node, frame: FnContext, nres: int, receivers: list | nil = nil) -> int | error:
    args := (_tail(node, 2) catch nil)
    if args is nil:
        # `recv ! name()` - a send whose argument list is empty.
        args = []
    if (receivers is nil) and has_spread_or_keyword(args):
        reloc := message_index(node, frame)
        if reloc is error:
            return reloc
        target_node := (_field(_msg_binding(node), 1) catch nil)
        return compile_va(frame, nres, 'msg_va, nil, args, reloc, target_node)
    argc := len(args)
    if argc > 128:
        return error(str(argc) + " arguments, over the 128-parameter call limit")
    reloc := message_index(node, frame)
    if reloc is error:
        return reloc
    base := frame ! mark()
    window := frame ! push()
    if window is error:
        return window
    if receivers is nil:
        target_node := (_field(_msg_binding(node), 1) catch nil)
        r := compile_expr(target_node, frame, window)
        if r is error:
            return r
        # The receiver lands in `window`, but computing it (`a.b.c`) may
        # have left temporaries above it; the arguments must start at
        # window + 1. pypoc gets this for free by parsing `(a.b.c) ! m()`
        # as a one-receiver message tuple, which frees its own temps.
        fw := frame ! free_to(window + 1)
        if fw is error:
            return fw
    else:
        # `(a, b) ! name(...)`: the receivers are tupled first, and the
        # tuple is the receiver the VM dispatches on (spec 7.1).
        inner := frame ! mark()
        for item in receivers:
            w := compile_into_window(item, frame)
            if w is error:
                return w
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'tuple, a0=window, a1=L(inner), f=len(receivers))
        if e is error:
            return e
        fi := frame ! free_to(inner)
        if fi is error:
            return fi
    for arg in args:
        w2 := compile_into_window(arg, frame)
        if w2 is error:
            return w2
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, 'msg, a0=window, f=argc, a1=reloc, a2=nres)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    held := base + _max2(nres, 1)
    while (frame ! mark()) < held:
        p := frame ! push()
        if p is error:
            return p
    fr := frame ! free_to(held)
    if fr is error:
        return fr
    return window

# See deviation 5: a `'tuple` target subsumes ast.MessageTupleExpr.
# The tree carries a fifth field (`parser.wy`'s $_mk_postfix): 'bound for
# the bare `recv ! name` form - the bound closure, not a dispatch - and
# 'send for a call, whose argument list may still be empty (`recv ! name()`).
fn _message(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    target_node := (_field(_msg_binding(node), 1) catch nil)
    if node_kind(node) == 'bind_msg:
        # `recv ! name` with no call: the bound closure, not a dispatch.
        recv := compile_expr(target_node, frame, nil)
        if recv is error:
            return recv
        reloc := message_index(node, frame)
        if reloc is error:
            return reloc
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getmsg, a0=reg, a1=recv, a2=reloc)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if node_kind(target_node) == 'tuple:
        items := (_tail(target_node, 1) catch nil)
        receivers := []
        for it in items:
            receivers ! append(it)
        r := compile_message(node, frame, 1, receivers)
        if r is error:
            return r
        return _land(frame, dst, r)
    r2 := compile_message(node, frame, 1, nil)
    if r2 is error:
        return r2
    return _land(frame, dst, r2)

# `a::b::c` reads a namespace member. Three cases, in order: a runtime
# value on the left is a dynamic lookup (`getscope`); a module-local
# class's own static is the module global it was allotted (spec 4.7);
# anything else is a name from outside this module and so a free global
# slotreg, never resolved at compile time (spec 6.3). See deviation 9 for how
# this differs from pypoc's per-level recursive check.
fn _scope(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    parts := []
    rest := (_tail(node, 1) catch nil)
    while rest is pair:
        parts ! append(str(car(rest)))
        rest = cdr(rest)
    if len(parts) == 0:
        return error("empty qualified name")
    # `name::$ast` (epic 10a M0): the leaf spelling marks a definition-tree
    # reference, not a scope hop. `$ast` is an ordinary name to the parser
    # (`$` is a legal identifier character), so the decision belongs here,
    # where the whole path is in hand, and is taken only when a module
    # actually says `::$ast` - a module that never does records nothing and
    # pays nothing.
    if parts[len(parts) - 1] == AST_FIELD:
        return _astref(parts, frame, dst)
    first := sym(parts[0])
    if (frame ! lookup(first)) is nil:
        gidx := (frame.module) ! class_static(parts)
        if not (gidx is nil):
            reg := _target(frame, dst)
            if reg is error:
                return reg
            e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'gget, gidx, reg)
            if e is error:
                return e
            return reg
        if not ((frame.module) ! is_local_class(parts[0])):
            # `b::V` after `import a::b` names `a::b::V` (M2).
            parts = (frame.module) ! canonical_path(parts)
            free := (frame.module) ! declare_free_global(_join_parts(parts))
            if free is error:
                return free
            return _land_free(frame, dst, free)
    obj := resolve_name(first, frame, nil)
    if obj is error:
        return obj
    i := 1
    n := len(parts)
    reg := obj
    while i < n:
        symbol := (frame.module.image) ! add_symbol(parts[i])
        if symbol is error:
            return symbol
        var target: int
        if i == (n - 1):
            target = _target(frame, dst)
        else:
            target = frame ! push()
        if target is error:
            return target
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'getscope, a0=target, a1=reg, a2=symbol)
        if e is error:
            return e
        reg = target
        i = i + 1
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `name::$ast` - see deviation 8. `AST_FIELD` is the leaf spelling; `_astref`
# resolves it against `module.definitions` and materializes the tree.

AST_FIELD := "$ast"

# `name::$ast` - the tree of the definition `name` names, as a value
# (pypoc's `_ast_ref`, resolved against `module.definitions`; the same
# "describes the definition, not the binding" rule its docstring records -
# a local variable could never hold a tree, so no lexical scope is consulted
# here). Only a two-component path names a definition: `a::b::$ast` has a
# scope hop in the middle, and trees do not live in scopes. The tree is
# emitted at the reference site through `emit_value` - the same
# tree-constant path `$[...]` literals take - so it is built only for the
# definitions a module actually reaches for.
fn _astref(parts: list, frame: FnContext, dst):
    if len(parts) != 2:
        return error("`::$ast` needs the name of a definition in this module")
    name := parts[0]
    definition := (frame.module.definitions[name] catch nil)
    if definition is nil:
        return error("`" + name + "::$ast` names no fn, co or class defined in this module; a compiled module carries no trees to reach across")
    return emit_value(definition, frame, dst)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# coroutines (spec 7.1)

fn compile_yield(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    if not frame.is_coroutine:
        return error("`yield` is only meaningful inside a `co`")
    value_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    base := frame ! mark()
    var count: int
    if value_node is nil:
        p := frame ! push()
        if p is error:
            return p
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=p)
        if e is error:
            return e
        count = 1
    elif node_kind(value_node) == 'tuple:
        items := (_tail(value_node, 1) catch nil)
        for item in items:
            w := compile_into_window(item, frame)
            if w is error:
                return w
        count = len(items)
    else:
        w2 := compile_into_window(value_node, frame)
        if w2 is error:
            return w2
        count = 1
    if count > 128:
        return error(str(count) + " yielded values, over the 128-result limit")
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, 'yield, a0=L(base), f=count)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    fr := frame ! free_to(base + 1)
    if fr is error:
        return fr
    return _land(frame, dst, L(base))

fn _yield(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    return compile_yield(node, frame, dst)

fn _yield_from(node, frame: FnContext, dst):
    if not frame.is_coroutine:
        return error("`yield` is only meaningful inside a `co`")
    value_node := (_field(node, 1) catch nil)
    sub := compile_expr(value_node, frame, nil)
    if sub is error:
        return sub
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, 'yield_from, a0=reg, a1=sub)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# spreads and keyword arguments (spec 7.1)

# Build one `tuple`/`dict` from a run of expressions; `count` is the
# element count for a tuple/list and the *pair* count for a dict (the
# caller tracks it - wyrm has no floor-division operator to derive it from
# `len(items)` here).
fn _collection(frame: FnContext, op, items: list, count: int, dst):
    base := frame ! mark()
    for item in items:
        w := compile_into_window(item, frame)
        if w is error:
            return w
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    e := _emit_pack(frame, op, a0=reg, a1=L(base), f=count)
    if e is error:
        return e
    return reg

# Fold segments together with `add` - "runtime concat" (spec 7.1) is `add`,
# the VM dispatches it through the `__add__` family. A single segment folds
# to itself, so the common no-spread case emits no extra instruction.
fn _concatenate(frame: FnContext, segments: list):
    result := segments[0]
    i := 1
    n := len(segments)
    while i < n:
        joined := frame ! push()
        if joined is error:
            return joined
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'add, a0=joined, a1=result, a2=segments[i])
        if e is error:
            return e
        result = joined
        i = i + 1
    return result

fn _positional_argument_tuple(args, frame: FnContext):
    segments := []
    run := []
    for arg in args:
        kind := node_kind(arg)
        if kind == 'spread:
            if len(run) > 0:
                c := _collection(frame, 'tuple, run, len(run), nil)
                if c is error:
                    return c
                segments ! append(c)
                run = []
            value_node := (_field(arg, 1) catch nil)
            v := compile_expr(value_node, frame, nil)
            if v is error:
                return v
            segments ! append(v)
        elif not ((kind == 'kwarg) or (kind == 'spread_kw)):
            run ! append(arg)
    if (len(run) > 0) or (len(segments) == 0):
        c2 := _collection(frame, 'tuple, run, len(run), nil)
        if c2 is error:
            return c2
        segments ! append(c2)
    return _concatenate(frame, segments)

fn _keyword_argument_dict(args, frame: FnContext):
    segments := []
    run := []
    pairs := 0
    for arg in args:
        kind := node_kind(arg)
        if kind == 'spread_kw:
            if len(run) > 0:
                c := _collection(frame, 'dict, run, pairs, nil)
                if c is error:
                    return c
                segments ! append(c)
                run = []
                pairs = 0
            value_node := (_field(arg, 1) catch nil)
            v := compile_expr(value_node, frame, nil)
            if v is error:
                return v
            segments ! append(v)
        elif kind == 'kwarg:
            name := (_field(arg, 1) catch nil)
            value_node := (_field(arg, 2) catch nil)
            run ! append($['str, str(name)])
            run ! append(value_node)
            pairs = pairs + 1
    if (len(run) > 0) or (len(segments) == 0):
        c2 := _collection(frame, 'dict, run, pairs, nil)
        if c2 is error:
            return c2
        segments ! append(c2)
    return _concatenate(frame, segments)

# `f(*a, key=1, **k)` and its message twin. The window is fixed at three
# registers - callee (or receiver), the positional tuple, the keyword dict
# - so every spread shape reaches the VM the same way (spec 7.1).
# `callee_node` is nil when `receiver_node` is given (the message form) and
# vice versa.
fn compile_va(frame: FnContext, nres: int, op, callee_node, args, reloc, receiver_node) -> int | error:
    base := frame ! mark()
    window := frame ! push()
    if window is error:
        return window
    positional_slot := frame ! push()
    if positional_slot is error:
        return positional_slot
    keyword_slot := frame ! push()
    if keyword_slot is error:
        return keyword_slot
    if receiver_node is nil:
        if callee_node is sym:
            r := resolve_name(callee_node, frame, window)
            if r is error:
                return r
        else:
            r2 := compile_expr(callee_node, frame, window)
            if r2 is error:
                return r2
    else:
        r3 := compile_expr(receiver_node, frame, window)
        if r3 is error:
            return r3
    scratch := frame ! mark()
    positional := _positional_argument_tuple(args, frame)
    if positional is error:
        return positional
    e1 := _emit_pairable(frame, 'move, positional_slot, positional)
    if e1 is error:
        return e1
    f1 := frame ! free_to(scratch)
    if f1 is error:
        return f1
    keywords := _keyword_argument_dict(args, frame)
    if keywords is error:
        return keywords
    e2 := _emit_pairable(frame, 'move, keyword_slot, keywords)
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    f2 := frame ! free_to(scratch)
    if f2 is error:
        return f2
    if op == 'call_va:
        e3 := _emit_pack(frame, op, a0=window, a1=nres)
        if e3 is error:
            return e3
    else:
        e4 := _emit_pack(frame, op, a0=window, a1=reloc, a2=nres)
        if e4 is error:
            return e4
    held := base + _max2(nres, 3)
    while (frame ! mark()) < held:
        p := frame ! push()
        if p is error:
            return p
    f3 := frame ! free_to(base + _max2(nres, 1))
    if f3 is error:
        return f3
    return window

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# constant materialization - see deviation 8. `_astref` (above) resolves
# `name::$ast` and lands here: `emit_value` is the tree-constant primitive,
# and is simpler here than in pypoc because a wy/wyrm tree node already *is*
# the native sexpr-shaped value (pair lists of syms/literals) - there is no
# `sexpr.encode()` step to run first, `value` is taken as-is.

fn _emit_sequence(items: list, frame: FnContext, dst, op) -> int | error:
    reg := _target(frame, dst)
    if reg is error:
        return reg
    base := frame ! mark()
    for item in items:
        slotreg := frame ! push()
        if slotreg is error:
            return slotreg
        e := emit_value(item, frame, slotreg)
        if e is error:
            return e
    a1 := reg
    if len(items) > 0:
        a1 = L(base)
    e2 := _emit_pack(frame, op, a0=reg, a1=a1, f=len(items))
    if e2 is error:
        return e2
    fr := frame ! free_to(base)
    if fr is error:
        return fr
    return reg

# Emit code building a plain wyrm data value: a wy/wyrm tree node already
# IS this shape (symbols, strings, numbers, bools, nil, lists and proper
# pair lists), so unlike expressions.py there is no separate `sexpr.encode`
# step - `value` is taken as-is.
fn emit_value(value, frame: FnContext, dst):
    if value is sym:
        index := (frame.module.image) ! add_symbol(str(value))
        if index is error:
            return index
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'lsym, index, reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if value is nil:
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'lnil, a0=reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if value is bool:
        return _bool_lit(value, frame, dst)
    if value is int:
        if (value < INT32_MIN) or (value > INT32_MAX):
            return error("integer " + str(value) + " in a tree does not fit in an i32")
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'i8, reg, value)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if value is float:
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        bits := _f32_bits(value)
        e := _emit_pack(frame, 'f32, a0=reg, w1=bits)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if value is str:
        index := (frame.module.image) ! add_static(value)
        if index is error:
            return index
        reg := _target(frame, dst)
        if reg is error:
            return reg
        e := _emit_pairable(frame, 'lconst, index, reg)
        if e is error:
            return e
        return reg
    if value is list:
        return _emit_sequence(value, frame, dst, 'list)
    if value is pair:
        items := []
        node := value
        while node is pair:
            items ! append(car(node))
            node = cdr(node)
        if not (node is nil):
            return error("a pair with an improper tail has no bytecode form; only proper pair lists do")
        return _emit_sequence(items, frame, dst, 'plist)
    return error("value has no bytecode form; a tree can only hold symbols, strings, numbers, bools, nil, lists and pair lists")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the entry point

# Lower one expression, returning the register holding its value (or an
# `error`). See the module docstring for the `dst` contract.
fn compile_expr(node, frame: FnContext, dst = nil):
    if node is sym:
        return _bare_symbol(node, frame, dst)
    if not (node is pair):
        return error("not an expression: " + str(node))
    kind := node_kind(node)
    if kind is nil:
        return error("not an expression: " + str(node))
    ks := str(kind)
    if ks == "str":
        return _string(node, frame, dst)
    elif (ks == "int") or (ks == "float"):
        return _number(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "char":
        return _char_lit(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "sym":
        return _symbol_lit(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "nil":
        return _nil_lit(frame, dst)
    elif ks == "true":
        return _bool_lit(true, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "false":
        return _bool_lit(false, frame, dst)
    elif (ks == "break") or (ks == "continue") or (ks == "pass"):
        return error(ks + " is not an expression")
    elif ks == "not":
        return _unary(node, frame, dst, 'not)
    elif ks == "neg":
        return _unary(node, frame, dst, 'neg)
    elif ks == "~":
        return _unary(node, frame, dst, 'inv)
    elif ks == "pos":
        return _pos(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "**":
        return _exp(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "and":
        return _short_circuit(node, frame, dst, true)
    elif ks == "or":
        return _short_circuit(node, frame, dst, false)
    elif ks == "in":
        return _in_op(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "is":
        return _type_check(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks in BINARY_OPERATORS:
        return _binop(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "apply":
        if node_kind((_field(node, 1) catch nil)) == 'bind_msg:
            return _message(node, frame, dst)
        return _call(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "bind_msg":
        return _message(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "array":
        return _array(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "tuple":
        return _tuple(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "list":
        return _plist(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "dict":
        return _dict(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "attr":
        return _attr(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "index":
        return _index(node, frame, dst)
    elif (ks == "lambda") or (ks == "co_lambda"):
        return _lambda(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "do":
        return _do(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "try":
        return _try(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "catch":
        return _catch(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "yield":
        return _yield(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "cond":
        # `if` is an expression as well as a statement (wyrm.gram's
        # if_expr): the same node either way, lowered by the registered
        # statement-shape handler (statements.wy's _if_expr, the same
        # lowering with the branch values landing in `dst`).
        return dispatch(EXPR_HANDLERS, node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "yield_from":
        return _yield_from(node, frame, dst)
    elif ks == "::":
        return _scope(node, frame, dst)
    else:
        return error("the bytecode compiler does not support '" + ks + "' expressions yet")
